<h1 id=3D"AI-323:AI+IntegrationofMilvus-Introduction">Introduction</h1>
<p>The landscape of Artificial Intelligence (AI) is increasingly shaped by =
the efficient management and retrieval of complex, high-dimensional data. V=
ector databases have emerged as crucial components in this evolution, parti=
cularly for applications leveraging semantic similarity and intricate data =
representations such as image and facial embeddings. These specialized data=
bases excel at storing and querying vector data, enabling sophisticated tas=
ks that are challenging for traditional relational databases. The ability t=
o perform fast and accurate similarity searches within vast collections of =
vectors has made them indispensable for a wide range of AI applications, in=
cluding recommendation systems, natural language processing, and, significa=
ntly, image and facial recognition.</p>
<p>Face recognition, at its core, is fundamentally a problem of vector simi=
larity search. In this paradigm, facial images are transformed into high-di=
mensional numerical vectors, known as embeddings, which capture the unique =
characteristics of each face. The process of recognizing a face then involv=
es comparing a query embedding against a database of known facial embedding=
s to find the closest match based on a chosen similarity metric. The effect=
iveness of a face recognition system hinges on the quality of these embeddi=
ngs and, critically, the ability of the underlying vector database to effic=
iently search and retrieve similar embeddings at scale.</p>
<p>As face recognition systems are deployed in increasingly diverse and dem=
anding scenarios, the need to handle large and growing datasets becomes par=
amount. Systems transitioning from a few thousand to hundreds of thousands =
or even millions of facial embeddings require a robust and scalable vector =
database infrastructure. Maintaining high performance and accuracy under su=
ch scaling demands presents significant challenges, making the choice of ve=
ctor database a critical decision. The selected database must not only stor=
e a large volume of vector data but also provide efficient indexing and sea=
rch mechanisms to ensure timely and accurate recognition results.</p>
<p>One of the missions of AI+ is to provide best-in-class service with open=
 source and scalable resources. The Dodgers project specifically requires a=
 facial recognition system capable of handling a substantial number of embe=
ddings (potentially exceeding 300,000) to effectively recognize players, st=
aff, and other individuals in various contexts. In the space of open-source=
 vector database solutions, this leaves two prominent candidates: Milvus an=
d ChromaDB. Both databases are designed to manage and query vector embeddin=
gs, but they differ significantly in their architecture, features, and suit=
ability for various use cases. ChromaDB is often characterized as a lightwe=
ight vector database that prioritizes ease of use and development-friendlin=
ess, and was initially used in our existing facial recognition implementati=
on. In contrast, Milvus is engineered as a purpose-built vector database, e=
xcelling in handling large-scale, high-performance applications with low-la=
tency requirements. The primary objective of this report is to evaluate the=
se two databases across key criteria relevant to building a scalable face r=
ecognition system. By examining their performance, scalability, features, o=
perational aspects, and cost considerations, this analysis will provide a w=
ell-founded justification for selecting the most appropriate vector databas=
e for the user's specific needs.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI-323:AI+IntegrationofMilvus-VectorDatabaseArchitecture">Vector =
Database Architecture</h1>
<p>When considering a face recognition project that needs to scale beyond p=
roof-of-concept volumes, the performance of the underlying vector database =
at larger scales becomes a critical factor. Our initial facial recognition =
implementation using ChromaDB started with approximately 3K embeddings, but=
 quickly revealed the need for 10-20K embeddings (roughly 1,000 per player)=
 to achieve reliable recognition results. Milvus is specifically architecte=
d to excel in handling large-scale vector search operations, capable of man=
aging datasets containing billions or even trillions of vector points. Its =
distributed architecture allows it to distribute the workload across multip=
le nodes, enabling horizontal scaling and maintaining performance as the da=
ta volume increases. This design makes Milvus well-suited for applications =
with rapidly growing datasets, ensuring low latency and high throughput eve=
n with substantial data. The ability of Milvus to dynamically allocate new =
nodes further enhances its scalability and resource management capabilities=
. Evidence suggests that Milvus can efficiently handle large-scale embeddin=
gs, as this falls well within its stated capacity for large-scale data mana=
gement.</p>
<p>Conversely, ChromaDB, while proficient in managing vector data, is gener=
ally considered a better fit for developers working with smaller datasets. =
Our own experience with the existing facial recognition implementation usin=
g ChromaDB revealed significant index management challenges as we scaled fr=
om the initial 3K embeddings to the necessary 10-20K embeddings needed for =
reliable player recognition. Official ChromaDB documentation provides a spe=
cific constraint, linking collection size directly to available RAM. For in=
stance, a t3.small EC2 instance with 2GB of RAM is expected to handle appro=
ximately 250,000 embeddings of 1024 dimensions. ChromaDB's documentation pr=
ovides a formula to estimate maximum collection size: N =3D R * 0.245, wher=
e N is the maximum collection size in millions and R is the RAM in GB for 1=
024-dimensional embeddings.</p>
<p>This RAM-dependent model indicates a vertical scaling approach, where ca=
pacity increases require upgrading a single machine's resources rather than=
 distributing across multiple nodes. The documentation further notes that s=
ingle-node ChromaDB is fundamentally single-threaded, and query latency inc=
reases as collections grow. Most critically, "If a collection grows larger =
than available memory, insert and query latency spike rapidly as the operat=
ing system begins swapping memory to disk... and the system quickly becomes=
 unusable." This creates a significant reliability risk for large-scale pro=
jects if memory capacity is exceeded.</p>
<p></p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap">
<table data-table-width=3D"760" data-layout=3D"default" data-local-id=3D"e1=
453954-f297-48d9-9999-f8d738be5de0" class=3D"confluenceTable">
<tbody>
