<p>The availability of native GPU acceleration in Milvus provides a distinc=
t performance advantage for a face recognition system aiming for 300K+ embe=
ddings. GPU acceleration is particularly beneficial for latency-sensitive a=
pplications like real-time face recognition, where quick and accurate resul=
ts are essential. The lack of native GPU support in ChromaDB could be a lim=
iting factor in achieving the desired performance levels at this scale, esp=
ecially if real-time processing is a requirement.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI-323:AI+IntegrationofMilvus-DeploymentandOperations">Deployment=
 and Operations</h1>
<h2 id=3D"AI-323:AI+IntegrationofMilvus-DeploymentArchitecture">Deployment =
Architecture</h2>
<p>Scalability and performance for large datasets often necessitate a distr=
ibuted architecture. Milvus is engineered for large-scale, distributed envi=
ronments, offering elastic and horizontal scalability. This means that as t=
he data volume or query load increases, new nodes can be added to the clust=
er to handle the increased demands, ensuring low latency and high throughpu=
t. Milvus supports distributed deployment via Kubernetes, which simplifies =
scaling and management. Key components of a Milvus on-premise cluster inclu=
de the Proxy (for request handling), Coordinator (for metadata and cluster =
orchestration), Data Nodes (for vector storage), Index Nodes (for index man=
agement), and Query Nodes (for search execution). Optimal performance in a =
distributed Milvus deployment requires high-bandwidth, low-latency network =
connections and fast storage solutions like SSDs or NVMe.</p>
<p>In contrast, ChromaDB is primarily a single-node solution. While this ar=
chitecture can be simpler to deploy and manage for smaller applications, it=
 inherently limits scalability. As the dataset size grows, a single node ca=
n become a bottleneck for both storage and computation. The lack of built-i=
n distributed data replacement in ChromaDB further hinders its ability to s=
cale effectively for applications with rapidly increasing data demands. Alt=
hough ChromaDB is working on a distributed and serverless architecture for =
its cloud offering, the current single-node nature of the open-source versi=
on presents a significant limitation for a face recognition project aiming =
for 300K+ embeddings and potential future growth.</p>
