<p>Milvus's support for sharding and partitioning provides a mechanism for =
efficiently managing a large face recognition dataset and potentially impro=
ving query performance by distributing the data and workload. The lack of b=
uilt-in sharding in ChromaDB's single-node architecture could pose challeng=
es for very large datasets beyond the immediate 300K target and for impleme=
nting multi-tenancy if the application's scope were to expand significantly=
.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-323:AI+IntegrationofMilvus-CostConsiderations">Cost Considerat=
ions</h2>
<p>Resource management represents an important consideration when deploying=
 a vector database. For our project, the available GPUs are currently dedic=
ated to inferencing tasks, making them unavailable for vector database oper=
ations. This resource constraint is a significant factor in planning for fu=
ture scaling.</p>
<p>Milvus offers flexibility in deployment models, including CPU-only confi=
gurations that can be adopted initially and expanded to leverage GPU accele=
ration as resources become available. On-premise deployments of Milvus requ=
ire careful planning for hardware resources, particularly for distributed d=
eployments. (The Zilliz Cloud option provides a potential alternative that =
could offload infrastructure management if needed in the future.)</p>
<p>ChromaDB, being open-source and primarily designed for single-node deplo=
yments, has lower initial infrastructure requirements but can face resource=
 constraints as datasets grow. As experienced in our existing implementatio=
n, scaling beyond a modest number of embeddings quickly demanded additional=
 memory resources. For on-premises deployments, cost remains a secondary co=
ncern compared to performance and scalability needs.</p>
